00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
It's finally time to deploy your climate_analyzer workflow

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.200
with a professional web interface.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.800
You'll learn how to serve NAT workflows as HTTP and WebSocket APIs.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.381
launch the NeMo Agent Toolkit UI

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.750
and interact with agents in a polished production ready setup.

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:19.120
Let's have some fun. Now that we've built our climate agent

00:00:19.221 --> 00:00:21.521
and we've composed it together with our calculator agent,

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:24.420
we are ready to serve it in a production ready workflow.

00:00:24.821 --> 00:00:27.803
In this lesson, we're going to deploy our workflow as an API.

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:29.402
and we're going to connect to the

00:00:29.402 --> 00:00:31.003
NeMo Agent Toolkit UI to that API

00:00:31.003 --> 00:00:32.302
and we're going to be able to

00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:33.603
chat with it in a human interface.

00:00:34.003 --> 00:00:36.302
We've already seen this command, nat serve.

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:40.203
This hosts a FastAPI server and is ready to go

00:00:40.203 --> 00:00:43.603
with WebSocket support, OpenAPI docs and health checks.

00:00:44.402 --> 00:00:46.082
An API server is great. Now we

00:00:46.082 --> 00:00:48.003
can run a UI on top of that

00:00:48.003 --> 00:00:50.003
and chat with it using natural language.

00:00:50.524 --> 00:00:52.823
Okay, we built a powerful agent.

00:00:53.223 --> 00:00:56.124
We've wired in observability, we've run evals against it.

00:00:56.323 --> 00:00:58.323
Now let's put a UI on top of it so

00:00:58.323 --> 00:01:00.524
we can actually begin to use it in the real world.

00:01:00.835 --> 00:01:03.636
In this notebook, we're going to serve our agent as an API.

00:01:03.835 --> 00:01:05.128
We're going to connect the UI

00:01:05.128 --> 00:01:06.635
that we saw on the first notebook,

00:01:06.835 --> 00:01:08.585
and we're going to see what an

00:01:08.585 --> 00:01:10.335
API first architecture looks like with agents.

00:01:11.036 --> 00:01:12.789
First, let's install our climate_analyzer just

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:14.835
to make sure it's ready to go.

00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:19.835
As we've seen in previous lessons, normally to serve an API

00:01:19.936 --> 00:01:22.835
of your agentic workflow is just NAT Serve.

00:01:23.197 --> 00:01:24.597
But since we're going to be running

00:01:24.597 --> 00:01:26.197
it in the background in a Jupyter notebook,

00:01:26.197 --> 00:01:27.747
there's a lot of extra code that goes

00:01:27.747 --> 00:01:29.298
around it to run it as a subprocess.

00:01:29.498 --> 00:01:31.498
Don't get too bogged down with that.

00:01:31.498 --> 00:01:33.498
Just focus on the fact that we're running NAT serve

00:01:33.498 --> 00:01:35.697
against our config file, like we've seen in the past.

00:01:36.159 --> 00:01:38.659
And the API server is running.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:42.459
Next, we're going to pull down the NeMo Agent Toolkit UI.

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:45.459
The UI is a separate open source repo,

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:47.859
and that's because you might have your own

00:01:47.859 --> 00:01:49.959
UI and we didn't want to cross dependencies.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:51.735
But you can pull it down to your system and

00:01:51.735 --> 00:01:53.358
run it, which is what we're going to do right now.

00:01:53.453 --> 00:01:56.253
The NAT repository is pulled and ready to go.

00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:58.390
So now that we've pulled the UI and

00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:00.242
we've got the dependencies installed, let's start it up.

00:02:01.352 --> 00:02:04.453
So on the command line you would run npm run dev,

00:02:04.453 --> 00:02:07.052
as this is just a standard npm project.

00:02:07.253 --> 00:02:08.752
Of course, we have to run it

00:02:08.752 --> 00:02:10.252
as a subprocess in this Jupyter notebook.

00:02:10.836 --> 00:02:13.336
So we've got it running and it is launched.

00:02:13.637 --> 00:02:15.937
And here we have some helper script

00:02:15.937 --> 00:02:18.437
that will give us the proper URL to your UI.

00:02:18.736 --> 00:02:21.236
It is running locally on port 3000

00:02:21.514 --> 00:02:23.715
but depending on where your Jupyter notebook is hosted,

00:02:24.014 --> 00:02:27.215
the URL might be different. And so this gives us that URL.

00:02:27.615 --> 00:02:30.014
All right, and let's open up this UI and check it out.

00:02:30.615 --> 00:02:32.615
And now we're ready to chat with our climate agent.

00:02:33.514 --> 00:02:35.314
Let's start with one of our suggested questions.

00:02:35.514 --> 00:02:38.014
What was Mexico's average temperature 1990 to 2000

00:02:38.014 --> 00:02:39.715
versus global?

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:41.883
You might remember that we looked

00:02:41.883 --> 00:02:43.930
at this UI in the first notebook

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.280
and it was just coming up with whatever

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.629
the LLM happened to have in its training.

00:02:46.629 --> 00:02:48.629
But now our climate agent has tools

00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:50.430
that can pull from real world data.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:52.930
And it has access to a calculator

00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:54.930
so it can do calculations on that data.

00:02:55.224 --> 00:02:57.324
So let's see how it does with this question.

00:02:57.724 --> 00:03:00.224
So for our question, we can see the agent successfully ran

00:03:00.724 --> 00:03:02.638
and gave us the correct answer. And

00:03:02.638 --> 00:03:04.824
we can also look at the intermediate steps.

00:03:05.125 --> 00:03:07.324
For instance, we can see the chain of thought

00:03:07.724 --> 00:03:10.724
as the agent called various tools like calculate_statistics.

00:03:10.919 --> 00:03:13.118
and the data that it passed into that function

00:03:13.118 --> 00:03:15.218
and the data that it got from that function.

00:03:15.318 --> 00:03:17.368
So this is a way for us to

00:03:17.368 --> 00:03:19.419
evaluate our agents in a live chat environment

00:03:19.419 --> 00:03:21.419
and we can ask a follow-up question.

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:25.118
What was the average temperature specifically

00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:27.639
in 2000 in Mexico?

00:03:28.438 --> 00:03:30.192
So we can chat over our

00:03:30.192 --> 00:03:32.239
data in a more realistic, human setting.

00:03:32.938 --> 00:03:37.026
Let's ask another question. This question's a little bit more complex.

00:03:37.026 --> 00:03:38.947
It asks for a complete climate

00:03:38.947 --> 00:03:41.187
analysis for France with trends and visualization.

00:03:41.887 --> 00:03:45.586
So we can see it did this complex climate analysis for France

00:03:45.586 --> 00:03:48.986
and it generated a visualization that it dropped into a repository.

00:03:49.510 --> 00:03:52.411
The UI is complex and has a lot of additional features.

00:03:52.811 --> 00:03:55.311
For instance, we can stream results in real time

00:03:55.411 --> 00:03:57.211
and watch the agent think instead of just

00:03:57.211 --> 00:03:59.010
getting the results back all in one go.

00:03:59.311 --> 00:04:02.411
And you can talk to the UI using HTTP streaming or WebSockets

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.311
or standard HTTP. And this UI is just one example.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.510
Any front end can connect to your API.

00:04:09.068 --> 00:04:10.515
Now that we've taken a look at

00:04:10.515 --> 00:04:12.169
the UI and ran it against our agent,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:14.769
let's clean everything up so we're ready to go.

00:04:15.269 --> 00:04:18.468
And we're done. So we have built a climate analyzer.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:21.168
It connects to real-world data sources.

00:04:21.536 --> 00:04:24.935
It combines multiple agents, one written in LangGraph,

00:04:25.036 --> 00:04:27.235
another written in plain Python functions.

00:04:27.536 --> 00:04:29.536
We served it with a production API.

00:04:29.836 --> 00:04:33.036
We ran evaluations against it, plugged it into observability,

00:04:33.336 --> 00:04:35.536
and we put a UI in front of that API

00:04:35.636 --> 00:04:37.336
and proved that we can chat with our agent.
